data.content:
Your code **does not use `Thread.Abort`**, `Thread.ResetAbort`, or catch `ThreadAbortException`. The APIs it does use (`Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, `Thread.Sleep`) work identically in .NET 8 — no migration is needed for abort-related concerns.

**However**, for a .NET 8 migration, the idiomatic improvement would be replacing raw threads with `Task.WhenAll` + cooperative cancellation:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken ct = default)
    {
        var tasks = items.Select(item => Task.Run(() => Process(item, ct), ct));
        await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

This gives you cancellation support via `CancellationToken` (the modern replacement for `Thread.Abort`) without needing it today — but you'll be ready if callers ever need to cancel work.
